@inject NavigationManager Navigation

@* The way back from the foot of a long table. A table of stored rows is the one thing on this site
   that can run several screens, and its foot is where a reader ends up: the pager, the rows-per-page
   picker and the end of the data are all down there, while the heading, the count and the narrowing
   that produced it are back at the top.

   Under the card rather than in it, for the same reason the count above it is: both are the page
   speaking about the table rather than part of it, and the card is the filter row, the table and
   the footer closed into one shape that a fourth piece would open again.

   It lands where a page link lands — see <see cref="PageTop"/> — because there is one beginning of
   the page and not two: the bar, above the breadcrumb and the heading alike. The address is written
   out rather than left as the fragment alone: the document carries a base href, and a bare "#top"
   is resolved against that rather than against the page being read, which would send a reader to
   the home page. Writing it out costs nothing else, since it is the address the page is already at,
   the way the refresh link above the card is.

   A link rather than a button with script behind it, like every other control on these pages: it
   works with scripting off, it can be opened in a new tab, and the bar it lands on is focusable, so
   a reader on the keyboard carries on from the top of the page rather than from the foot they
   left. *@
<a class="back-to-top" href="@Here#@PageTop.Anchor">
    <TopMark/>
    Back to top
</a>

@code {

    /// <summary>
    /// The address this page is being read at, relative to the base so it is written the way every
    /// other link here is. The query string comes with it, which is what keeps the link a move
    /// within this page rather than a move to the unnarrowed first page of it.
    /// </summary>
    private string Here => Navigation.ToBaseRelativePath(Navigation.Uri);

}
